1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,662
Chapter 60: Diabetes Mellitus.

3
00:00:05,662 --> 00:00:27,925
Types Of Diabetes Pregestational Diabetes Diagnosis Fetal Effects Maternal Effects Management In Pregnancy Gestational Diabetes Screening And Diagnosis Maternal And Fetal Effects Management References According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2020), nearly 27 million adults in the United States have been diagnosed with diabetes.

4
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:35,737
Another 7.3 million are suspected to be undiagnosed, and an estimated 88 million have prediabetes.

5
00:00:35,737 --> 00:00:50,662
Reasons for these substantial rates include an aging population, which is more likely to develop type 2 diabetes; population growth within minority groups at particular risk for type 2 diabetes; and a dramatic rise in obesity rates.

6
00:00:50,662 --> 00:00:57,237
In 2019, almost three in 10 women were considered obese prior to becoming pregnant .

7
00:00:57,237 --> 00:01:08,800
The strong relationship between diabetes and the current obesity epidemic in the United States underlines the critical need for diet and lifestyle interventions to change the trajectory of both.

8
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,037
Types Of Diabetes.

9
00:01:11,037 --> 00:01:18,800
In nonpregnant individuals, the type of diabetes is based on its presumed pathogenesis and its manifestations.

10
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:26,575
Absolute insulin deficiency, which generally is autoimmune in etiology, characterizes type 1 diabetes.

11
00:01:26,575 --> 00:01:35,737
In contrast, insulin resistance, relative insulin deficiency, or elevated glucose production characterizes type 2 diabetes .

12
00:01:35,737 --> 00:01:43,425
Both types are generally preceded by a period of abnormal glucose homeostasis often referred to as prediabetes.

13
00:01:43,425 --> 00:01:51,987
Pancreatic β-cell destruction can begin at any age, but type 1 diabetes is clinically apparent most often before age 30.

14
00:01:51,987 --> 00:01:59,625
Type 2 diabetes usually develops with advancing age but is increasingly identified in younger obese adolescents.

15
00:01:59,625 --> 00:02:12,487
Etiological overlap in diabetes subtypes is well established and has led to the proposal of a single classification system centered on β-cell function along with the concept of individualized treatment strategies .

16
00:02:12,487 --> 00:02:17,987
Other forms of diabetes include maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY).

17
00:02:17,987 --> 00:02:21,687
The more common MODY type is in obese adolescents .

18
00:02:21,687 --> 00:02:30,425
The less common form is an autosomal dominant condition and characterized by mild diabetes diagnosed in adolescence or young adulthood .

19
00:02:30,425 --> 00:02:32,762
Table 60-1.

20
00:02:32,762 --> 00:02:39,112
Etiological Classification of Diabetes Mellitus # Classification During Pregnancy.

21
00:02:39,112 --> 00:02:43,587
Diabetes is the most common medical complication of pregnancy.

22
00:02:43,587 --> 00:02:55,137
Women can be separated into those diagnosed with diabetes before pregnancy— pregestational or overt diabetes, and those diagnosed during pregnancy— gestational diabetes.

23
00:02:55,137 --> 00:03:08,237
The proportion of pregnancies complicated by diabetes more than doubled between 1994 and 2008, after which rates have leveled (Deputy, 2018; Jovanovič, 2015).

24
00:03:08,237 --> 00:03:15,362
In 2018, 7.6 percent of pregnant women in this country had some form of diabetes .

25
00:03:15,362 --> 00:03:23,350
Prevalence is highest among non-Hispanic blacks, Mexican-Americans, Puerto Rican-Americans, and Native Americans .

26
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:26,150
# White Classification.

27
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:36,137
Until the mid-1990s, the classification system of Priscilla White (1978) for diabetic pregnant women was the linchpin of management.

28
00:03:36,137 --> 00:03:45,175
Today, the White classification is used less often but still provides simple, useful information on pregnancy risks and prognosis .

29
00:03:45,175 --> 00:04:00,000
Because most currently cited literature also contains data from these older classifications, the one previously recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (1986) is shown in Table 60-2.

30
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,312
Table 60-2.

31
00:04:02,312 --> 00:04:17,862
Modified White Classification Scheme Used from 1986 Through 1994 for Diabetes Complicating Pregnancy The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) no longer recommends the White classification.

32
00:04:17,862 --> 00:04:24,312
The current focus is whether diabetes antedates pregnancy or is first diagnosed during pregnancy.

33
00:04:24,312 --> 00:04:29,712
Many now recommend the system proposed by the American Diabetes Association .

34
00:04:29,712 --> 00:04:32,062
Table 60-3.

35
00:04:32,062 --> 00:04:38,012
Proposed Classification System for Diabetes in Pregnancy Pregestational Diabetes.

36
00:04:38,012 --> 00:04:49,887
Considering the previously mentioned high percentage of undiagnosed diabetes, many women identified with gestational diabetes likely have previously unrecognized type 2 diabetes.

37
00:04:49,887 --> 00:04:52,850
Indeed, 5 to 10 percent of

38
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:59,607
women with gestational diabetes are diagnosed with overt diabetes immediately after pregnancy.

39
00:04:59,557 --> 00:05:01,957
# Diagnosis.

40
00:05:01,957 --> 00:05:09,495
Women with high plasma glucose levels, glucosuria, and ketoacidosis present no diagnostic challenge.

41
00:05:09,495 --> 00:05:45,545
Women with a random plasma glucose level >200 mg/dL plus classic signs and symptoms such as polydipsia, polyuria, and unexplained weight loss, those with a fasting glucose level >125 mg/dL, or those with a first-trimester glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1c) level of ≥6.5 percent are considered by the American Diabetes Association (2019) and the World Health Organization (2019) to have overt diabetes first detected in pregnancy.

42
00:05:45,545 --> 00:05:50,907
Women with only minimal metabolic derangement may be more difficult to identify.

43
00:05:50,907 --> 00:06:11,932
To diagnose overt diabetes in pregnancy, the International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups (IADPSG) Consensus Panel (2010) recognizes the threshold values found in Table 60-4 for fasting or random plasma glucose and HbA1c levels at prenatal care initiation.

44
00:06:11,932 --> 00:06:29,445
The American Diabetes Association (2020) and the World Health Organization (2019) now also consider a plasma glucose level >200 mg/dL measured 2 hours after a 75-g oral glucose load to be diagnostic.

45
00:06:29,445 --> 00:06:37,232
No consensus has been reached as to whether such testing should be universal or limited to those women classified as high risk.

46
00:06:37,232 --> 00:06:39,595
Table 60-4.

47
00:06:39,595 --> 00:06:50,245
Diagnosis of Overt Diabetes in Pregnancya Regardless, a presumed diagnosis of overt diabetes in pregnancy based on these thresholds should be confirmed postpartum.

48
00:06:50,245 --> 00:07:03,845
Risk factors for impaired carbohydrate metabolism in pregnant women include a strong familial history of diabetes, prior delivery of a large newborn, persistent glucosuria, or unexplained fetal losses.

49
00:07:03,845 --> 00:07:06,432
# Harm in Pregnancy.

50
00:07:06,432 --> 00:07:15,595
With overt diabetes, the embryo, fetus, and mother frequently experience serious complications directly attributable to diabetes .

51
00:07:15,595 --> 00:07:22,720
Many of these complications might be prevented each year by preconceptional care for improved glycemic control .

52
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:40,795
Using HbA1c values as objective risk quartiles, Finneran and Kiefer (2020) reported that glycemic control throughout pregnancy and a late-pregnancy HbA1c level <6.5 percent leads to reduced rates of adverse obstetrical and neonatal outcomes.

53
00:07:40,795 --> 00:07:48,257
The likelihood of successful outcomes with overt diabetes, however, is not simply related to glucose control.

54
00:07:48,257 --> 00:07:53,682
The degree of underlying cardiovascular or renal disease may be more important.

55
00:07:53,682 --> 00:08:02,832
Thus, advancing stages of the White classification, seen in Table 60-2, are inversely related to favorable pregnancy outcomes.

56
00:08:02,832 --> 00:08:11,445
Shown in Table 60-5 are data that chronicle the adverse pregnancy outcomes with overt diabetes, and many are described next .

57
00:08:11,445 --> 00:08:13,932
Table 60-5.

58
00:08:13,932 --> 00:08:23,745
Selected Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes in Percent in Women with Gestational and Overt Diabetes Compared with Nondiabetic Womena # Fetal Effects.

59
00:08:23,745 --> 00:08:33,670
Spontaneous Abortion Up to 25 percent of overtly diabetic mothers have an early miscarriage, and poor glycemic control is an associated factor.

60
00:08:33,670 --> 00:08:47,370
In one study, those whose HbA1c concentrations were >12 percent or whose preprandial glucose concentrations persisted above 120 mg/dL had an elevated miscarriage risk .

61
00:08:47,370 --> 00:09:01,270
In a large Chinese population-based study, those with a history of diabetes had an increased risk of miscarriage, and the risk rose 8 percent for each 20-mg/dL incremental rise in fasting glucose .

62
00:09:01,270 --> 00:09:11,970
In 89 pregnancies in women with the monogenic form of MODY, only women with the causative glucose kinase (GCK) mutation were more likely to miscarry .

63
00:09:11,970 --> 00:09:20,582
Another analysis of 128 GCK-MODY pregnancies showed that the observed miscarriage rate was comparable to the background rate .

64
00:09:20,582 --> 00:09:25,657
These women have hyperglycemic variability that can be difficult to control.

65
00:09:25,657 --> 00:09:31,520
Preterm Delivery Overt diabetes is an undisputed risk factor for preterm birth.

66
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:45,870
In an analysis of more than 500,000 pregnancies in Ontario, almost 20 percent of women with diabetes were delivered preterm compared with 5.6 percent of women without diabetes, obesity, or hypertension .

67
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:53,257
More than 60 percent were indicated preterm births, that is, due to obstetrical or medical complications.

68
00:09:53,257 --> 00:09:54,832
Notably,

69
00:09:54,988 --> 00:10:01,338
more than 37 percent of women with diabetes and chronic hypertension delivered preterm.

70
00:10:01,288 --> 00:10:12,138
In a review of California births, 19 percent of women with pregestational diabetes delivered before 37 weeks’ gestation compared with 9 percent of controls .

71
00:10:12,138 --> 00:10:23,163
In a study from the United Kingdom, the preterm delivery rate was 42 percent for 8690 type 1 diabetic women and 3 percent for those with type 2 diabetes .

72
00:10:23,163 --> 00:10:37,125
Malformations The incidence of major malformations in fetuses of women with type 1 diabetes approximates 11 percent and is at least double the rate in fetuses of nondiabetic mothers (Jovanovič, 2015).

73
00:10:37,125 --> 00:10:44,150
This risk is present for all women with pregestational diabetes, including those with type 2 disease .

74
00:10:44,150 --> 00:10:50,275
Congenital anomalies constitute almost half of perinatal deaths in diabetic pregnancies.

75
00:10:50,275 --> 00:11:01,913
Cardiovascular malformations account for more than half of the anomalies, and Table 60-6 lists selected malformations reported by the National Birth Defects Prevention Study .

76
00:11:01,913 --> 00:11:13,213
In a study of more than 2 million births in Canada, the risk of an isolated cardiac defect was fivefold higher in women with type 1 diabetes compared with nondiabetic mothers .

77
00:11:13,213 --> 00:11:27,525
The caudal regression sequence, described in Chapter 15 (p. 280), is a rare malformation that, according to the National Birth Defects Prevention Study, is 80 times more likely in women with pregestational diabetes.

78
00:11:27,525 --> 00:11:29,988
Table 60-6.

79
00:11:29,988 --> 00:11:44,163
Selected Congenital Malformations in Pregnancies Complicated by Overt Diabetes Poorly controlled diabetes, both preconceptionally and early in pregnancy, is thought to underlie this elevated risk for major malformation.

80
00:11:44,163 --> 00:12:00,000
In one study of mothers with type 1 diabetes, a periconceptional HbA1c <6.5 percent carried a twofold risk for a fetal major cardiac defect, and a HbA1c >9 percent carried a sixfold risk .

81
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,850
Others cite similar frequencies .

82
00:12:02,850 --> 00:12:14,138
Figure 60-1 shows the association between HbA1c levels and fetal congenital malformation rates in a Parkland Hospital cohort of women with overt diabetes .

83
00:12:14,138 --> 00:12:16,475
Figure 60-1.

84
00:12:16,475 --> 00:12:28,663
Association between fetal malformation rates and HbA1c values determined at initiation of prenatal care in 1573 pregnancies in women with pregestational diabetes.

85
00:12:28,663 --> 00:12:47,163
The etiological mechanisms that explain this link include excess production of toxic superoxide radicals, altered cell signaling pathways, upregulation of some genes by hyperglycemia, and activation of programmed cell death (Basu, 2018; Reece, 2012).

86
00:12:47,163 --> 00:12:58,375
One review of potential molecular mechanisms suggests that cellular responses to oxidative stress represent possible therapeutic targets to prevent diabetes-induced embryopathy .

87
00:12:58,375 --> 00:13:07,925
Altered Fetal Growth Diminished fetal growth may result from congenital malformations or from substrate deprivation due to advanced maternal vascular disease.

88
00:13:07,925 --> 00:13:13,300
That said, fetal overgrowth is more typical of pregestational diabetes.

89
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:21,000
Maternal hyperglycemia prompts fetal hyperinsulinemia, and this in turn stimulates excessive somatic growth.

90
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:28,663
Except for the brain, most fetal organs are affected by the macrosomia that characterizes the fetus of diabetic women.

91
00:13:28,663 --> 00:13:39,763
Newborns are described as anthropometrically different from other large- for-gestational age (LGA) neonates (Catalano, 2003; Durnwald, 2004).

92
00:13:39,763 --> 00:13:50,350
Specifically, those whose mothers are diabetic have excessive fat deposition on the shoulders and trunk, which predisposes to shoulder dystocia or fetopelvic disproportion .

93
00:13:50,350 --> 00:13:52,650
Figure 60-2.

94
00:13:52,650 --> 00:13:58,588
This 6050-g macrosomic neonate was born to a woman with gestational diabetes.

95
00:13:58,588 --> 00:14:08,438
The incidence of macrosomia rises significantly when mean maternal blood glucose concentrations chronically exceed 130 mg/dL .

96
00:14:08,438 --> 00:14:16,563
In addition, the overall birthweight distribution of neonates of diabetic mothers is skewed toward consistently heavier birthweights.

97
00:14:16,563 --> 00:14:29,000
In one sonographic study, the macrosomia rates for Nordic women with type 1, type 2, or gestational diabetes were 35, 28, and 24 percent, respectively .

98
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:35,138
Moreover, the abdominal circumference grew disproportionately larger in the diabetic groups.

99
00:14:35,138 --> 00:14:44,750
Analysis of head circumference/abdominal circumference (HC/AC) ratios shows that this disproportionate growth occurs mainly in

100
00:14:44,908 --> 00:14:49,620
diabetic pregnancies that ultimately yield macrosomic newborns.

101
00:14:49,570 --> 00:15:01,333
Unexplained Fetal Demise The risk of fetal death is three to four times higher in women with pregestational diabetes (Gardosi, 2013; Patel, 2015).

102
00:15:01,333 --> 00:15:18,408
In the United Kingdom study of more than 17,000 pregnancies in women with pregestational diabetes, the stillbirth incidence was similar in those with type 1 or type 2 diabetes—10.4 versus 13.5 per 1000 live and stillbirths, respectively .

103
00:15:18,408 --> 00:15:26,420
Stillbirth without an identifiable cause is a phenomenon relatively limited to pregnancies complicated by overt diabetes.

104
00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:38,345
These stillbirths are “unexplained” because common factors such as obvious placental insufficiency, placental abruption, fetal-growth restriction, or oligohydramnios are not identified.

105
00:15:38,345 --> 00:15:44,858
These fetuses are typically LGA and die before labor, usually later in the third trimester.

106
00:15:44,858 --> 00:15:50,383
These unexplained stillbirths are usually associated with poor glycemic control.

107
00:15:50,383 --> 00:15:59,295
In one study, suboptimal glycemic control was identified in two thirds of unexplained stillbirths between 1990 and 2000 .

108
00:15:59,295 --> 00:16:04,745
Fetuses of diabetic mothers also often have elevated lactic acid levels.

109
00:16:04,745 --> 00:16:19,370
Salvesen and colleagues (1992, 1993) analyzed fetal blood samples and reported that mean umbilical venous blood pH was lower in diabetic pregnancies and was significantly related to fetal insulin levels.

110
00:16:19,370 --> 00:16:30,345
These findings support a hypothesis that hyperglycemic-mediated chronic aberrations in oxygen and fetal metabolite transport may underlie these unexplained fetal deaths .

111
00:16:30,345 --> 00:16:34,383
Maternal ketoacidosis also can cause fetal death .

112
00:16:34,383 --> 00:16:44,808
Moreover, stillbirths from placental insufficiency occur with increased frequency in women with overt diabetes and are usually associated with severe preeclampsia.

113
00:16:44,808 --> 00:16:57,508
In the prior cited California study, the fetal death risk was sevenfold higher in women with hypertension and pregestational diabetes compared with the threefold increased risk associated with diabetes alone .

114
00:16:57,508 --> 00:17:04,058
Stillbirth rates are also elevated in women with advanced diabetes and vascular complications.

115
00:17:04,058 --> 00:17:10,108
Hydramnios Diabetic pregnancies are often complicated by excess amnionic fluid.

116
00:17:10,108 --> 00:17:33,208
In one sonographic study, 18 percent of 314 pregnancies complicated by pregestational diabetes were found to have hydramnios in the third trimester, which was defined as an amnionic fluid index (AFI) >24 cm. Women with elevated HbA1c values in the third trimester were more likely to have hydramnios .

117
00:17:33,208 --> 00:17:40,158
In a similar study, poor maternal glucose control was associated with macrosomia and hydramnios .

118
00:17:40,158 --> 00:17:47,058
A likely— albeit unproven—explanation is that fetal hyperglycemia causes fetal polyuria .

119
00:17:47,058 --> 00:17:58,033
In a study from Parkland Hospital, Dashe and coworkers (2000) found that the AFI parallels the amnionic fluid glucose level among women with diabetes.

120
00:17:58,033 --> 00:18:09,270
Neonatal Effects Before tests of fetal health and maturity became available, delivery before term was deliberately selected for women with diabetes to avoid unexplained stillbirth.

121
00:18:09,270 --> 00:18:17,120
Although this practice has been largely abandoned, a higher frequency of preterm delivery in women with diabetes persists.

122
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:30,733
One analysis of early-term delivery (370/7 to 386/7 weeks) showed a 13-percent reduction in such deliveries in women with diabetes between 2005 and 2011 .

123
00:18:30,733 --> 00:18:37,083
Most indicated deliveries are prompted by advanced diabetes with superimposed preeclampsia.

124
00:18:37,083 --> 00:18:47,620
Although modern neonatal care has reduced neonatal death rates due to immaturity, neonatal morbidity due to preterm birth continues to be a serious consequence.

125
00:18:47,620 --> 00:19:06,645
In one Neonatal Research Network study of 10,781 extremely preterm neonates, those born to diabetic women treated with insulin prior to pregnancy were at greater risk for necrotizing enterocolitis and late-onset sepsis than similarly aged neonates of mothers without diabetes .

126
00:19:06,645 --> 00:19:16,145
Respiratory Distress Syndrome Gestational age rather than overt diabetes is likely the most significant factor associated with respiratory distress syndrome .

127
00:19:16,145 --> 00:19:30,295
Indeed, in one analysis of 19,399 very-low-birthweight neonates delivered between 24 and 33 weeks’ gestation, rates of respiratory distress syndrome in newborns of diabetic mothers were

128
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:35,110
not higher compared with rates in neonates of nondiabetic mothers .

129
00:19:35,060 --> 00:19:42,222
Diabetes does not appear to alter the beneficial effects of antenatal corticosteroids to hasten lung maturity .

130
00:19:42,222 --> 00:19:51,385
Hypoglycemia and Hypocalcemia Newborns of a diabetic mother experience a rapid drop in plasma glucose concentration after delivery.

131
00:19:51,385 --> 00:19:58,372
This is attributed to the hyperplasia of fetal βislet cells that is induced by chronic maternal hyperglycemia.

132
00:19:58,372 --> 00:20:11,910
Low glucose concentrations—defined as <45 mg/dL—are particularly common in newborns of women with unstable glucose concentrations during labor (van Kempen, 2020).

133
00:20:11,910 --> 00:20:20,235
Preliminary observations showed that early-term fetuses exposed to antenatal corticosteroids have a high rate of hypoglycemia .

134
00:20:20,235 --> 00:20:27,360
Frequent blood glucose measurements in the newborn and active early feeding practices can mitigate this complication.

135
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:35,510
Hypocalcemia is defined as a total serum calcium concentration <8 mg/dL in term newborns.

136
00:20:35,510 --> 00:20:42,510
Early-onset hypocalcemia is one of the potential metabolic derangements in neonates of diabetic mothers.

137
00:20:42,510 --> 00:20:44,747
Its cause is unclear.

138
00:20:44,747 --> 00:20:51,072
Theories include altered magnesium–calcium economy, asphyxia, and preterm birth.

139
00:20:51,072 --> 00:21:00,147
In one randomized study, 137 pregnant women with type 1 diabetes were managed with strict versus customary glucose control .

140
00:21:00,147 --> 00:21:08,897
Almost a third of neonates in the customary control group developed hypocalcemia compared with only 18 percent of those in the strict-control group.

141
00:21:08,897 --> 00:21:17,385
Hyperbilirubinemia and Polycythemia The pathogenesis of hyperbilirubinemia in neonates of diabetic mothers is uncertain.

142
00:21:17,385 --> 00:21:23,497
A major contributing factor is newborn polycythemia, which raises the bilirubin load .

143
00:21:23,497 --> 00:21:28,472
Polycythemia is thought to be a fetal response to relative hypoxia.

144
00:21:28,472 --> 00:21:40,047
According to Hay (2012), the sources of this fetal hypoxia are hyperglycemia-mediated elevations in maternal affinity for oxygen and fetal oxygen consumption.

145
00:21:40,047 --> 00:21:48,510
Together with insulin-like growth factors, this hypoxia leads to elevated fetal erythropoietin levels and red cell production.

146
00:21:48,510 --> 00:21:56,972
Cardiomyopathy Newborns of pregestational and gestational diabetic pregnancies frequently have hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.

147
00:21:56,972 --> 00:22:04,947
This remodeling can be associated with cardiac dysfunction (Aguilera, 2020; Depla, 2021).

148
00:22:04,947 --> 00:22:15,322
Huang and coworkers (2013) propose that pathological ventricular hypertrophy in neonates born to women with diabetes is due to insulin excess.

149
00:22:15,322 --> 00:22:21,147
In severe cases, this cardiomyopathy may lead to obstructive cardiac failure.

150
00:22:21,147 --> 00:22:29,697
In one study, the fetuses of 26 women with pregestational diabetes underwent serial echocardiographic evaluation.

151
00:22:29,697 --> 00:22:35,497
In the first trimester, fetal diastolic dysfunction was already evident in some.

152
00:22:35,497 --> 00:22:43,710
In the third trimester, the fetal interventricular septum and right ventricular wall were thicker in fetuses of diabetic mothers .

153
00:22:43,710 --> 00:22:46,860
Others report similar observations .

154
00:22:46,860 --> 00:22:54,635
Most affected newborns are asymptomatic following birth, and hypertrophy usually resolves in the months after delivery.

155
00:22:54,635 --> 00:22:58,610
Any long-term adverse sequelae remain to be determined .

156
00:22:58,610 --> 00:23:05,485
Long-term Cognitive Development Intrauterine metabolic conditions are linked to neurodevelopment in offspring.

157
00:23:05,485 --> 00:23:15,597
In a study of more than 700,000 Swedish men, the intelligence quotient of those whose mothers had diabetes during pregnancy averaged 1 to 2 points lower .

158
00:23:15,597 --> 00:23:24,210
DeBoer and associates (2005) demonstrated impaired memory performance at age 1 year in infants of diabetic mothers.

159
00:23:24,210 --> 00:23:37,060
Results from the Childhood Autism Risks from Genetics and the Environment (CHARGE) study indicated that autism spectrum disorders or developmental delay also were more common in children of diabetic women .

160
00:23:37,060 --> 00:23:49,435
Adane and colleagues (2016) confirmed a consistent relationship between maternal diabetes and diminished cognitive and language development in studies of younger children but not older children.

161
00:23:49,435 --> 00:24:04,022
Because interpreting effects of the intrauterine environment on neurodevelopment is confounded by postnatal factors, the link between maternal diabetes, glycemic control, and long-term neurocognitive outcome remains unconfirmed.

162
00:24:04,022 --> 00:24:10,835
Inheritance The risk of developing type 1 diabetes if either parent is affected is 3 to 5 percent.

163
00:24:10,835 --> 00:24:13,622
Type 2 diabetes has a much

164
00:24:13,780 --> 00:24:16,317
stronger genetic component.

165
00:24:16,267 --> 00:24:23,142
If both parents have type 2 diabetes, the risk of their offspring developing it approaches 40 percent .

166
00:24:23,142 --> 00:24:30,817
Both types of diabetes develop after a complex interplay between genetic predisposition and environmental factors.

167
00:24:30,817 --> 00:24:42,780
Type 1 diabetes is prompted by environmental triggers such as infection, diet, or toxins and is heralded by the appearance of islet-cell autoantibodies in genetically vulnerable people .

168
00:24:42,780 --> 00:24:45,342
# Maternal Effects.

169
00:24:45,342 --> 00:24:51,417
Diabetes and pregnancy can interact to an extent that maternal welfare can be jeopardized.

170
00:24:51,417 --> 00:25:00,405
With the possible exception of diabetic retinopathy, however, the long-term course of diabetes does not appear to be affected by pregnancy.

171
00:25:00,405 --> 00:25:15,542
In one analysis of more than 800,000 pregnancies, the 1125 mothers with type 1 diabetes had greater risks for hypertension and respiratory complications than nondiabetic gravidas (Jovanovič, 2015).

172
00:25:15,542 --> 00:25:29,117
The 10,126 mothers with type 2 diabetes had an elevated risk for hypertension, infection, depression, and cardiac or respiratory complications compared with nondiabetic gravidas.

173
00:25:29,117 --> 00:25:36,530
Maternal death is uncommon, but rates in women with diabetes are still higher than those in unaffected gravidas.

174
00:25:36,530 --> 00:25:45,155
In one analysis of 972 women with type 1 diabetes, the maternal mortality rate was 0.5 percent.

175
00:25:45,155 --> 00:25:51,942
Deaths stemmed from diabetic ketoacidosis, hypoglycemia, hypertension, and infection .

176
00:25:51,942 --> 00:26:00,417
Preeclampsia Pregnancy-associated hypertension is the complication that most often forces preterm delivery in diabetic women.

177
00:26:00,417 --> 00:26:07,680
The incidence of chronic and gestational hypertension—and especially preeclampsia—is remarkably increased .

178
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:17,255
In one metaanalysis of 92 studies, women with pregestational diabetes had a nearly fourfold higher pooled relative risk for preeclampsia .

179
00:26:17,255 --> 00:26:25,367
In the prior cited California study, preeclampsia developed three to four times more often in women with overt diabetes.

180
00:26:25,367 --> 00:26:33,730
Moreover, those diabetics with coexistent chronic hypertension were almost 12 times more likely to develop preeclampsia .

181
00:26:33,730 --> 00:26:42,217
Women with type 1 diabetes in more advanced White classes typically exhibit preexisting vascular complications and nephropathy.

182
00:26:42,217 --> 00:26:46,105
These women are more likely to develop preeclampsia .

183
00:26:46,105 --> 00:26:55,667
This rising risk may be related to oxidative stress, which plays a key role in the pathogenesis of diabetic complications and preeclampsia.

184
00:26:55,667 --> 00:27:11,255
With this in mind, the Diabetes and Preeclampsia Intervention Trial (DAPIT) randomly assigned 762 women with type 1 diabetes to antioxidant vitamin C and E supplementation or placebo in the first half of pregnancy .

185
00:27:11,255 --> 00:27:17,592
Preeclampsia rates were not improved except in the few with a low antioxidant status at baseline.

186
00:27:17,592 --> 00:27:19,980
Figure 60-3.

187
00:27:19,980 --> 00:27:29,217
Incidence of preeclampsia in 491 women with type 1 diabetes stratefied by diabetic class in Sweden and the United States.

188
00:27:29,217 --> 00:27:34,267
Data from Hansona, 1993; Sibaib, 2000.

189
00:27:34,267 --> 00:27:44,392
Preventively, low-dose aspirin prophylaxis is recommended in women at high risk of preeclampsia, which includes those with type 1 or type 2 diabetes .

190
00:27:44,392 --> 00:27:52,617
An 81-mg oral daily dose is initiated between 12 and 28 weeks’ gestation and is continued until delivery .

191
00:27:52,617 --> 00:27:59,255
Diabetic Nephropathy Diabetes is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease in the United States .

192
00:27:59,255 --> 00:28:10,067
Clinically detectable nephropathy begins with microalbuminuria, recognized as 30 to 300 mg of protein in a 24-hour urine collection specimen.

193
00:28:10,067 --> 00:28:14,917
This may manifest as early as 5 years after diabetes onset.

194
00:28:14,917 --> 00:28:24,980
Macroalbuminuria—more than 300 mg in a 24-hour collection specimen—develops in patients destined to have end-stage renal disease.

195
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:33,192
Hypertension almost invariably develops during this period, and renal failure ensues typically in the next 5 to 10 years.

196
00:28:33,192 --> 00:28:43,592
The incidence of overt proteinuria is nearly 30 percent in individuals with type 1 diabetes and ranges from 4 to 20 percent in those with type 2 diabetes .

197
00:28:43,592 --> 00:28:48,167
Regression is common, presumably from improved glucose control.

198
00:28:48,167 --> 00:28:54,180
Approximately 5 percent of gravidas with overt diabetes already have renal involvement .

199
00:28:54,180 --> 00:28:58,492
As many as 40 percent of these will develop preeclampsia .

200
00:28:58,492 --> 00:29:07,055
In addition, Ambia and associates (2020) reported that the rates of preterm delivery, preeclampsia, and fetal-growth

201
00:29:07,204 --> 00:29:21,054
restriction were significantly higher in diabetic women with proteinuria >300 mg/d compared with rates in diabetic gravidas whose 24-hour protein excretion was <300 mg/d.

202
00:29:21,004 --> 00:29:26,041
In general, pregnancy does not appear to worsen diabetic nephropathy.

203
00:29:26,041 --> 00:29:34,416
In one prospective study of 43 women with diabetes, diabetic nephropathy did not progress through 12 months after delivery .

204
00:29:34,416 --> 00:29:38,179
Most of these women had only mild renal impairment.

205
00:29:38,179 --> 00:29:45,429
Conversely, pregnant women with moderate to severe renal impairment may have accelerated progression of their disease .

206
00:29:45,429 --> 00:29:58,329
As in women with glomerulopathies, hypertension or substantial proteinuria before or during pregnancy is a major predictive factor for ultimate progression to renal failure in women with diabetic nephropathy.

207
00:29:58,329 --> 00:30:06,429
Diabetic Retinopathy Retinal vasculopathy is a highly specific complication of both type 1 and type 2 diabetes.

208
00:30:06,429 --> 00:30:13,991
In the United States, diabetic retinopathy is the most important cause of visual impairment in working-aged adults.

209
00:30:13,991 --> 00:30:22,916
The first and most common visible lesions are small microaneurysms followed by blot hemorrhages that form when erythrocytes escape from the aneurysms.

210
00:30:22,916 --> 00:30:27,366
These areas leak serous fluid that creates hard exudates.

211
00:30:27,366 --> 00:30:32,004
Such features are termed background or nonproliferative retinopathy.

212
00:30:32,004 --> 00:30:38,466
With increasingly severe retinopathy, the abnormal vessels of background eye disease become occluded.

213
00:30:38,466 --> 00:30:43,891
This leads to retinal ischemia and infarctions that appear as cotton wool exudates.

214
00:30:43,891 --> 00:30:47,466
These are considered preproliferative retinopathy.

215
00:30:47,466 --> 00:30:54,929
In response to ischemia, neovascularization begins on the retinal surface and out into the vitreous cavity.

216
00:30:54,929 --> 00:30:58,029
Vision is obscured when these vessels bleed.

217
00:30:58,029 --> 00:31:05,029
Laser photocoagulation before hemorrhage reduces the rate of visual loss progression and blindness by half.

218
00:31:05,029 --> 00:31:09,279
The procedure may be performed during pregnancy when indicated.

219
00:31:09,279 --> 00:31:19,566
In one study of nearly 500 pregnancies in women with type 1 diabetes, a third had prepregnancy retinopathy and 16 percent of these showed worsening .

220
00:31:19,566 --> 00:31:26,091
Almost 25 percent of those without prepregnancy retinopathy developed disease during pregnancy.

221
00:31:26,091 --> 00:31:35,241
Fortunately, development of sight-threatening retinopathy was rare, and only 4 percent of patients followed postpartum had progression of disease.

222
00:31:35,241 --> 00:31:46,054
Another group of investigators evaluated 80 women with type 2 diabetes and identified retinopathy, mostly mild, in 14 percent during early pregnancy .

223
00:31:46,054 --> 00:31:49,566
Progression was identified in 14 percent.

224
00:31:49,566 --> 00:32:07,079
Other risk factors that have been associated with progression of retinopathy include hypertension, higher levels of insulin-like growth factor 1, placental growth factor, and macular edema identified in early pregnancy (Huang, 2015; Vestgaard, 2010).

225
00:32:07,079 --> 00:32:18,079
The American Academy of Ophthalmology (2019) recommends that pregnant women with overt diabetes should be offered retinal assessment after the first prenatal visit.

226
00:32:18,079 --> 00:32:24,416
Subsequent eye examinations depend on severity of retinopathy and level of glucose control.

227
00:32:24,416 --> 00:32:34,154
Currently, most agree that laser photocoagulation and good glycemic control during pregnancy minimize the potential for deleterious effects of pregnancy.

228
00:32:34,154 --> 00:32:41,516
Ironically, “acute” rigorous metabolic control during pregnancy has been linked to acute worsening of retinopathy.

229
00:32:41,516 --> 00:32:51,079
In a study of 201 women with retinopathy, almost 30 percent suffered eye disease progression during pregnancy despite intensive glucose control .

230
00:32:51,079 --> 00:33:03,366
That said, Wang and coworkers (1993) observed that although retinopathy worsened during the critical months of rigorous glucose control, long-term progression of eye disease actually slowed.

231
00:33:03,366 --> 00:33:10,854
Diabetic Neuropathy Peripheral, symmetrical sensorimotor diabetic neuropathy is uncommon in pregnant women.

232
00:33:10,854 --> 00:33:17,041
However, one form of this, diabetic gastropathy, can be troublesome during pregnancy.

233
00:33:17,041 --> 00:33:22,691
It causes nausea and vomiting, nutritional problems, and difficult glucose control.

234
00:33:22,691 --> 00:33:30,354
Affected women are advised that this complication is associated with a high risk of morbidity and poor perinatal outcome .

235
00:33:30,354 --> 00:33:36,629
Treatment with metoclopramide and dopamine D2 receptor antagonists is sometimes successful.

236
00:33:36,629 --> 00:33:41,091
Gastric neurostimulators have shown success during pregnancy .

237
00:33:41,091 --> 00:33:42,816
Treatment of

238
00:33:42,988 --> 00:33:53,288
hyperemesis gravidarum can be challenging, and we routinely provide insulin initially by continuous infusion for diabetic women who are admitted with this condition .

239
00:33:53,238 --> 00:34:04,338
Diabetic Ketoacidosis This serious complication develops in approximately 1 percent of diabetic pregnancies and is most often encountered in women with type 1 diabetes .

240
00:34:04,338 --> 00:34:14,813
It is increasingly being reported in women with type 2 or even those with gestational diabetes (Bryant, 2017; Sibai, 2014).

241
00:34:14,813 --> 00:34:31,100
Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) may develop with hyperemesis gravidarum, infection, insulin noncompliance, insulin pump failures, β-mimetic drugs given for tocolysis, and corticosteroids given to induce fetal lung maturation.

242
00:34:31,100 --> 00:34:38,513
DKA results from an insulin deficiency combined with an excess in counterregulatory hormones such as glucagon.

243
00:34:38,513 --> 00:34:43,038
This leads to gluconeogenesis and ketone body formation.

244
00:34:43,038 --> 00:34:53,963
The ketone body β-hydroxybutyrate is synthesized at a much greater rate than acetoacetate, which is preferentially detected by commonly used ketosis-detection methods.

245
00:34:53,963 --> 00:35:01,513
Thus, serum or plasma assays for β-hydroxybutyrate more accurately reflect true ketone body levels.

246
00:35:01,513 --> 00:35:16,825
The maternal mortality rate with DKA is <1 percent, but perinatal mortality rates associated with a single episode of DKA may reach 35 percent (Bryant, 2017; Guntupalli, 2015).

247
00:35:16,825 --> 00:35:25,238
Noncompliance is a prominent factor, and this and DKA were historically considered prognostically bad signs in pregnancy .

248
00:35:25,238 --> 00:35:32,638
Importantly, pregnant women usually develop DKA at lower blood glucose thresholds than when nonpregnant.

249
00:35:32,638 --> 00:35:44,600
In a study from Parkland Hospital, the mean glucose level for pregnant women with DKA was 380 mg/dL, and the mean HbA1C value was 10 percent .

250
00:35:44,600 --> 00:35:53,888
Euglycemic ketoacidosis during pregnancy also is possible but is rare (Sibai, 2014; Smati, 2020).

251
00:35:53,888 --> 00:36:00,138
One management protocol for diabetic ketoacidosis is shown in Table 60-7.

252
00:36:00,138 --> 00:36:08,150
An important cornerstone of management is vigorous rehydration with crystalloid solutions of normal saline or Ringer lactate.

253
00:36:08,150 --> 00:36:10,575
Table 60-7.

254
00:36:10,575 --> 00:36:19,100
Management of Diabetic Ketoacidosis During Pregnancy Infections The rates of many infections are higher in diabetic pregnant women.

255
00:36:19,100 --> 00:36:28,388
Common ones include candidal vulvovaginitis, bacterial urinary and respiratory tract infections, and puerperal pelvic sepsis.

256
00:36:28,388 --> 00:36:42,225
However, in one study of more than 1250 diabetic gravidas screened before 16 weeks’ gestation, rates of bacterial vaginosis or vaginal colonization with Candida or Trichomonas species were not increased .

257
00:36:42,225 --> 00:36:52,538
In one population-based study of almost 200,000 pregnancies, the risk of asymptomatic bacteriuria in women with diabetes was increased twofold .

258
00:36:52,538 --> 00:36:58,625
Another study found positive urine culture results in 25 percent of diabetic women .

259
00:36:58,625 --> 00:37:10,375
In a 2-year analysis of pyelonephritis in pregnant women at Parkland Hospital, 5 percent with diabetes developed pyelonephritis compared with 1.3 percent without diabetes .

260
00:37:10,375 --> 00:37:17,775
Rates of urinary tract infections can be minimized by screening and eradicating asymptomatic bacteriuria .

261
00:37:17,775 --> 00:37:30,063
Last, Johnston and colleagues (2017) reported that 16.5 percent of women with pregestational diabetes had postoperative wound complications following cesarean delivery.

262
00:37:30,063 --> 00:37:34,950
Prevention options are described in Chapter 30 (p. 550).

263
00:37:34,950 --> 00:37:37,725
# Preconceptional Care.

264
00:37:37,725 --> 00:37:48,450
Because of the close relationship between pregnancy complications and maternal glycemic control, efforts to achieve glucose targets are typically more aggressive during pregnancy.

265
00:37:48,450 --> 00:38:01,975
Unfortunately, nearly half of pregnancies in the United States are unplanned, and diabetic women frequently begin pregnancy with suboptimal glucose control (Finer, 2016; Kim, 2005).

266
00:38:01,975 --> 00:38:08,738
Management preferably should begin before pregnancy and then include specific goals during each trimester.

267
00:38:08,738 --> 00:38:21,263
To minimize early pregnancy loss and congenital malformations, the National Preconception Health and Healthcare Initiative Clinical Workgroup established values for optimal glycemic control before conception .

268
00:38:21,263 --> 00:38:28,913
This was defined as a HbA1c level <6.5 percent in women with pregestational diabetes.

269
00:38:28,913 --> 00:38:37,950
The American Diabetes Association (2017b) has also defined optimal preconceptional glucose control in those using

270
00:38:38,116 --> 00:38:39,703
insulin.

271
00:38:39,653 --> 00:38:57,928
Reflective values are self-monitored preprandial glucose levels of 70 to 100 mg/dL, peak 2-hour postprandial values of 100 to 120 mg/dL, and mean daily glucose concentrations <110 mg/dL.

272
00:38:57,928 --> 00:39:16,278
In one prospective population-based study of 933 pregnant women with type 1 diabetes, the risk of congenital malformations was not demonstrably higher with HbA1c levels <6.9 percent compared with the risk in more than 70,000 nondiabetic gravidas .

273
00:39:16,278 --> 00:39:26,278
However, a substantial fourfold greater risk for malformations was found for HbA1c levels >10 percent (see Fig. 60-1).

274
00:39:26,278 --> 00:39:33,666
Another study found fewer adverse outcomes with improved HbA1c values from conception to midpregnancy .

275
00:39:33,666 --> 00:39:42,828
If indicated, evaluation and treatment for diabetic complications such as retinopathy or nephropathy should be instituted before pregnancy.

276
00:39:42,828 --> 00:39:52,928
Last, folate, 400 μg/d orally, is given periconceptionally and during early pregnancy to decrease the risk of neural-tube defects .

277
00:39:52,928 --> 00:39:55,753
# First-trimester Care.

278
00:39:55,753 --> 00:39:59,416
Careful monitoring of glucose control is essential.

279
00:39:59,416 --> 00:40:10,266
For this reason, we routinely hospitalize women with overt diabetes during early pregnancy to initiate an individualized glucose control program and provide education.

280
00:40:10,266 --> 00:40:18,991
This also affords an opportunity to assess the extent of diabetic vascular complications and precisely establish gestational age.

281
00:40:18,991 --> 00:40:25,278
The checklist provided by the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) is helpful.

282
00:40:25,278 --> 00:40:39,391
Some initial evaluations done at Parkland Hospital include assessment of 24hour urine protein excretion and serum creatinine level, retinal examination, and echocardiogram if chronic hypertension is comorbid.

283
00:40:39,391 --> 00:40:55,203
First-trimester screening for aneuploidy may include measurement of maternal serum pregnancy-associated plasma protein A (PAPP-A), β-human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), and ultrasound measurement of fetal nuchal translucency .

284
00:40:55,203 --> 00:41:00,103
Noninvasive prenatal testing with cell-free DNA also is suitable.

285
00:41:00,103 --> 00:41:15,166
Although initially thought not to be different in women with pregestational diabetes, one analysis of more than 100 insulin-treated women identified reductions in median PAPP-A and β-hCG levels compared with gravidas without diabetes .

286
00:41:15,166 --> 00:41:20,716
Not surprisingly, they detected no difference in nuchal translucency measurements.

287
00:41:20,716 --> 00:41:27,041
When calculating aneuploidy risks in women with diabetes, these differences should be considered.

288
00:41:27,041 --> 00:41:29,553
# Insulin Treatment.

289
00:41:29,553 --> 00:41:33,841
The gravida with overt diabetes is best treated with insulin.

290
00:41:33,841 --> 00:41:46,841
Although oral hypoglycemic agents have been used successfully for gestational diabetes (p. 1083), these agents are not currently recommended for overt diabetes, despite some controversy .

291
00:41:46,841 --> 00:42:05,028
In an international, placebo-controlled trial including 502 insulin-treated women with type 2 diabetes, adjunctive metformin therapy was associated with improved glycemic control, reduced maternal weight gain, lower cesarean delivery rate, and less neonatal adiposity .

292
00:42:05,028 --> 00:42:19,116
Importantly, the proportion of small-for-gestational age newborns was higher in the insulin plus metformin group, but the rate of composite serious neonatal outcomes was not increased compared with those receiving insulin plus placebo.

293
00:42:19,116 --> 00:42:27,178
Maternal glycemic control can usually be achieved with multiple daily insulin injections and adjustment of dietary intake.

294
00:42:27,178 --> 00:42:33,191
Table 60-8 lists the action profiles of common short- and long-acting insulins .

295
00:42:33,191 --> 00:42:41,791
Subcutaneous insulin infusion by a calibrated pump does not yield better pregnancy outcomes compared with multiple daily injections.

296
00:42:41,791 --> 00:42:47,541
However, an infusion pump is a safe alternative in appropriately selected patients .

297
00:42:47,541 --> 00:43:05,891
With the advent of sensor-augmented insulin pumps and closed-loop insulin delivery systems, improved glycemic control with either manual or computer-generated insulin adjustments based on continuous glucose monitoring is now possible (Bergenstal, 2021; Stewart, 2016).

298
00:43:05,891 --> 00:43:16,041
In one study of insulin pump use in women with type 1 diabetes, total daily insulin doses declined in the first trimester but later rose more than threefold.

299
00:43:16,041 --> 00:43:21,841
Postprandial glucose elevations prompted most of the required daily-dose increases .

300
00:43:21,841 --> 00:43:25,578
If a continuous-infusion insulin pump is elected,

301
00:43:25,732 --> 00:43:34,069
initiation prepregnancy may help avoid hypoglycemia and ketoacidosis risks associated with the device’s learning curve .

302
00:43:34,019 --> 00:43:36,394
Table 60-8.

303
00:43:36,394 --> 00:43:47,294
Action Profiles of Commonly Used Insulins Monitoring Self-monitoring of capillary glucose levels using a glucometer is recommended because this involves the woman in her own care .

304
00:43:47,294 --> 00:43:59,769
The American Diabetes Association (2017b) recommends fasting and postprandial glucose monitoring, and Table 60-9 lists glucose goals recommended in pregnancy.

305
00:43:59,769 --> 00:44:08,094
Currently not standard care, one study showed that longitudinal HbA1c values could be used for risk stratification .

306
00:44:08,094 --> 00:44:15,894
Advances in noninvasive glucose monitoring will undoubtedly render intermittent capillary glucose monitoring obsolete.

307
00:44:15,894 --> 00:44:29,794
Subcutaneous continuous glucose monitoring devices have shown that pregnant women with diabetes experience significant periods of daytime hyperglycemia and nocturnal hypoglycemia that are undetected by traditional monitoring .

308
00:44:29,794 --> 00:44:40,969
Such glucose monitoring systems, coupled with a continuous insulin pump, offer the potential of an “artificial pancreas” to avoid hypo- or hyperglycemia during pregnancy .

309
00:44:40,969 --> 00:44:43,419
Table 60-9.

310
00:44:43,419 --> 00:45:01,569
Self-Monitored Capillary Blood Glucose Goals Diet Nutritional planning includes appropriate weight gain through carbohydrate and caloric modifications based on height, weight, and degree of glucose intolerance (American Diabetes Association, 2017b; Egan, 2020).

311
00:45:01,569 --> 00:45:09,419
The mix of carbohydrate, protein, and fat is adjusted to meet the metabolic goals and individual patient preferences.

312
00:45:09,419 --> 00:45:15,544
A minimum of 175 g/d of carbohydrates ideally is provided.

313
00:45:15,544 --> 00:45:28,619
In one analysis of more than 200 obese pregnant women with glucose intolerance, a lower carbohydrate intake, particularly late in pregnancy, was associated with lower fat mass in offspring at birth .

314
00:45:28,619 --> 00:45:35,969
Allotted carbohydrates are distributed throughout the day in three small- to moderate-sized meals and two to four snacks.

315
00:45:35,969 --> 00:45:43,182
Weight loss is not recommended, but modest caloric restriction may be appropriate for overweight or obese women.

316
00:45:43,182 --> 00:45:54,194
An ideal dietary composition is 55 percent carbohydrate, 20 percent protein, and 25 percent fat, of which <10 percent is saturated fat.

317
00:45:54,194 --> 00:46:03,682
Hypoglycemia Diabetic control can be unstable in the first half of pregnancy, and the incidence of hypoglycemia peaks during the first trimester.

318
00:46:03,682 --> 00:46:14,394
One study found hypoglycemic events—blood glucose values <40 mg/dL—in 37 of 60 women with type 1 diabetes.

319
00:46:14,394 --> 00:46:22,444
A fourth of these were considered severe because the women were unable to treat their own symptoms and required assistance from another person .

320
00:46:22,444 --> 00:46:29,494
Caution is recommended when attempting to achieve euglycemia in women with recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia.

321
00:46:29,494 --> 00:46:48,719
From one Cochrane database review, loose glycemic control, defined as fasting glucose values >120 mg/dL, was associated with greater risks for preeclampsia, cesarean delivery, and birthweight >90th percentile compared with women with tight or moderate control .

322
00:46:48,719 --> 00:47:00,069
With very tight control, defined by fasting values <90 mg/dL, no obvious benefits were gained, but the number of cases of hypoglycemia increased.

323
00:47:00,069 --> 00:47:02,944
# Second-trimester Care.

324
00:47:02,944 --> 00:47:15,482
Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein determination at 16 to 20 weeks’ gestation is used in association with targeted sonographic examination to detect neural-tube defects and other anomalies .

325
00:47:15,482 --> 00:47:22,044
These serum levels may be lower in diabetic pregnancies, and interpretation is altered accordingly.

326
00:47:22,044 --> 00:47:27,932
Because the incidence of congenital cardiac anomalies shown in Table 60-6.

327
00:47:27,932 --> 00:47:36,994
is increased fivefold in mothers with diabetes, fetal echocardiography is an important part of second-trimester sonographic evaluation .

328
00:47:36,994 --> 00:47:47,807
Dashe and coworkers (2009) cautioned that detection of fetal anomalies in obese diabetic women is more difficult than in similarly sized women without diabetes.

329
00:47:47,807 --> 00:47:54,769
Regarding second-trimester glucose control, normoglycemia with selfmonitoring continues to be the goal.

330
00:47:54,769 --> 00:48:00,119
After the first trimester’s glucose instability, a stable period ensues.

331
00:48:00,119 --> 00:48:03,882
This is then followed by higher insulin requirements.

332
00:48:03,882 --> 00:48:09,894
These stem from the enhanced peripheral resistance to insulin that is related to pregnancy and is

333
00:48:10,060 --> 00:48:13,760
described in Chapter 4 (p. 57).

334
00:48:13,710 --> 00:48:26,347
Although most women with pregestational diabetes require a higher total daily insulin dose with advancing gestational age, a small proportion will experience a reduction in their daily dose later in pregnancy.

335
00:48:26,347 --> 00:48:29,810
The significance of this drop remains uncertain.

336
00:48:29,810 --> 00:48:42,772
One Australian study evaluated women with falling insulin requirements (FIR), defined as a ≥15 percent drop in the peak total daily insulin dose after 20 weeks’ gestation .

337
00:48:42,772 --> 00:48:55,160
In 158 women with type 1 and type 2 diabetes and FIR, the risk for a composite of adverse outcomes indicative of placental insufficiency was fourfold greater than in those without FIR.

338
00:48:55,160 --> 00:49:06,535
The composite primary outcome included preeclampsia, fetal-growth disorders, delivery before 30 weeks’ gestation, placental abruption, and fetal death before 20 weeks.

339
00:49:06,535 --> 00:49:10,435
FIR was more common in women with type 1 diabetes.

340
00:49:10,435 --> 00:49:13,885
# Third-trimester Care and Delivery.

341
00:49:13,885 --> 00:49:25,247
During the past several decades, the threat of late-pregnancy stillbirth in women with diabetes prompted recommendations for various fetal surveillance programs beginning in the third trimester.

342
00:49:25,247 --> 00:49:36,047
Such protocols include fetal movement counting, periodic fetal heart rate monitoring, intermittent biophysical profile evaluation, and contraction stress testing .

343
00:49:36,047 --> 00:49:45,572
None of these techniques has been subjected to prospective randomized clinical trials, and their primary value seems related to their low false-negative rates.

344
00:49:45,572 --> 00:49:57,197
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c, 2021) suggests initiating such testing at 32 to 34 weeks’ gestation.

345
00:49:57,197 --> 00:50:06,597
With reassuring testing and no other complications, delivery is anticipated between 390/7 and 396/7 weeks.

346
00:50:06,597 --> 00:50:14,410
At Parkland Hospital, women with diabetes are seen in a specialized Maternal–Fetal Medicine clinic every 2 weeks.

347
00:50:14,410 --> 00:50:21,047
During these visits, glycemic control records are evaluated, and insulin doses are adjusted.

348
00:50:21,047 --> 00:50:27,135
Women are routinely instructed to perform fetal kick counts beginning early in the third trimester.

349
00:50:27,135 --> 00:50:34,410
At 34 weeks’ gestation, admission to our High-Risk Pregnancy Unit is offered to all insulin-treated women.

350
00:50:34,410 --> 00:50:37,885
Approximately half of these women choose admission.

351
00:50:37,885 --> 00:50:45,472
While in the hospital, they continue daily fetal movement counts and undergo fetal heart rate monitoring three times a week.

352
00:50:45,472 --> 00:50:51,585
With no other complications, delivery at Parkland is typically planned for 38 weeks.

353
00:50:51,585 --> 00:50:59,922
At the University of Alabama at Birmingham, weekly antenatal testing is initiated no later than 34 weeks’ gestation.

354
00:50:59,922 --> 00:51:07,935
Twice-weekly testing is reserved for those with poorly controlled diabetes or supervening medical or obstetrical complications.

355
00:51:07,935 --> 00:51:15,710
Delivery is planned for 39 weeks’ gestation in those with good glycemic control and reassuring antenatal testing.

356
00:51:15,710 --> 00:51:22,060
Earlier delivery is planned for those with poor glycemic control or significant comorbidities.

357
00:51:22,060 --> 00:51:30,435
For vaginal delivery, labor induction may be attempted when the fetus is not excessively large and the cervix is considered favorable .

358
00:51:30,435 --> 00:51:38,660
Cesarean delivery at or near term has frequently been used to avoid the traumatic birth of a large fetus in a woman with diabetes.

359
00:51:38,660 --> 00:51:51,410
In women with more advanced diabetes, especially those with vascular disease, the reduced likelihood of successful labor induction remote from term has also contributed to an increased cesarean delivery rate.

360
00:51:51,410 --> 00:52:04,372
In an analysis of pregnancy outcomes of diabetic women from University of Alabama at Birmingham according to the White classification, the rates of cesarean delivery and preeclampsia escalated with White class .

361
00:52:04,372 --> 00:52:13,947
In another study, a HbA1c level >6.4 percent at delivery was independently associated with urgent cesarean delivery .

362
00:52:13,947 --> 00:52:23,447
This suggests that tighter glycemic control during the third trimester might reduce late fetal compromise and cesarean delivery for fetal indications.

363
00:52:23,447 --> 00:52:27,660
Many of these women have undergone prior cesarean delivery.

364
00:52:27,660 --> 00:52:38,122
Somewhat related to this, the cesarean delivery rate for women with overt diabetes has remained at approximately 80 percent for the past 40 years at Parkland Hospital.

365
00:52:38,122 --> 00:52:43,985
Reducing or withholding the dose of long-acting insulin on the day of delivery is recommended.

366
00:52:44,140 --> 00:52:54,290
Regular insulin should be used to meet most or all of the insulin needs of the mother during this time, because insulin requirements typically drop markedly after delivery.

367
00:52:54,240 --> 00:53:01,227
During labor, continuous insulin infusion by calibrated intravenous pump is most satisfactory .

368
00:53:01,227 --> 00:53:08,690
The woman should be adequately hydrated intravenously and given glucose in sufficient amounts to maintain euglycemia.

369
00:53:08,690 --> 00:53:16,740
Capillary or plasma glucose levels are checked hourly during active labor, and regular insulin is administered accordingly.

370
00:53:16,740 --> 00:53:19,115
Table 60-10.

371
00:53:19,115 --> 00:53:25,327
Insulin Management for Labor Induction or Scheduled Cesarean Delivery # Puerperium.

372
00:53:25,327 --> 00:53:32,902
Often, many diabetic women may require virtually no insulin for the first 24 hours or more postpartum.

373
00:53:32,902 --> 00:53:38,802
Subsequently, insulin requirements may fluctuate markedly during the next few days.

374
00:53:38,802 --> 00:53:42,340
Infection must be promptly detected and treated.

375
00:53:42,340 --> 00:53:45,990
When appropriate, oral agents can be restarted.

376
00:53:45,990 --> 00:53:53,140
For type 2 diabetic women who will be transitioned to oral agents, this can be done on postoperative day 1.

377
00:53:53,140 --> 00:53:57,740
Counseling in the puerperium should include a discussion of birth control.

378
00:53:57,740 --> 00:54:06,490
Effective contraception is especially important in women with overt diabetes to allow optimal glucose control before subsequent conception .

379
00:54:06,490 --> 00:54:08,902
Gestational Diabetes.

380
00:54:08,902 --> 00:54:16,790
In the United States in 2016, almost 6 percent of pregnancies were complicated by gestational diabetes .

381
00:54:16,790 --> 00:54:25,977
Worldwide, its prevalence differs according to race, ethnicity, age, and body composition and by screening and diagnostic criteria.

382
00:54:25,977 --> 00:54:34,502
As discussed in the following sections, several persisting controversies pertain to the diagnosis and treatment of gestational diabetes.

383
00:54:34,502 --> 00:54:44,265
The word gestational implies that diabetes is induced by pregnancy— ostensibly because of exaggerated physiological changes in glucose metabolism .

384
00:54:44,265 --> 00:54:53,015
Gestational diabetes is defined as carbohydrate intolerance of variable severity with its onset or first recognition during pregnancy .

385
00:54:53,015 --> 00:55:02,402
This definition applies whether or not insulin is used for treatment and undoubtedly includes some women with previously unrecognized overt diabetes.

386
00:55:02,402 --> 00:55:11,365
The term gestational diabetes aims to communicate the need for enhanced surveillance during pregnancy and to stimulate further testing postpartum.

387
00:55:11,365 --> 00:55:19,202
The most important perinatal correlate is excessive fetal growth, which may result in both maternal and fetal birth trauma.

388
00:55:19,202 --> 00:55:26,965
The likelihood of fetal death with appropriately treated gestational diabetes is not different from that in the general population.

389
00:55:26,965 --> 00:55:34,177
More than half of women with gestational diabetes ultimately develop overt diabetes in the ensuing 20 years.

390
00:55:34,177 --> 00:55:42,127
Moreover, mounting evidence implicates long-range complications that include obesity and diabetes in their offspring.

391
00:55:42,127 --> 00:55:45,140
# Screening and Diagnosis.

392
00:55:45,140 --> 00:55:52,915
Despite almost 50 years of research, there is still no agreement on the optimal screening method for gestational diabetes.

393
00:55:52,915 --> 00:56:07,102
The difficulty in achieving consensus is underscored by the controversy following publication of the single-step approach shown in Table 60-11, which assesses the glucose values following a 75-g oral glucose load.

394
00:56:07,102 --> 00:56:23,627
This strategy is espoused by the International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Groups Consensus Panel (2010) and was greatly influenced by results of the Hypoglycemia and Pregnancy Outcomes (HAPO) study, described subsequently.

395
00:56:23,627 --> 00:56:40,165
Although the American Diabetes Association (2019) supports this new scheme, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) continues to recommend a two-step approach to screen and then diagnose gestational diabetes.

396
00:56:40,165 --> 00:56:51,365
Similarly, the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Consensus Development Conference in 2013 concluded that evidence is insufficient to adopt a one-step approach.

397
00:56:51,365 --> 00:56:53,765
Table 60-12.

398
00:56:53,765 --> 00:57:07,227
Risk-Based Recommended Screening Strategy for Detecting GDMa The recommended two-step approach begins with either universal or risk-based selective screening using a 50-g, 1-hour oral glucose challenge test.

399
00:57:07,227 --> 00:57:17,527
Participants in the Fifth International Workshop Conferences on Gestational Diabetes endorsed use of selective screening criteria shown in Table 60-12.

400
00:57:17,527 --> 00:57:20,677
Conversely, the American College of

401
00:57:20,836 --> 00:57:35,523
Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recommends universal screening of pregnant women with a protocol that provides a 50-g oral glucose load and that is followed in 1 hour by a laboratory-based blood glucose test.

402
00:57:35,473 --> 00:57:42,823
It is suggested that attempts to identify the minority of women who should not be screened would add unnecessary complexity.

403
00:57:42,823 --> 00:57:51,936
Screening should be performed between 24 and 28 weeks’ gestation in those women not known to have glucose intolerance earlier in pregnancy.

404
00:57:51,936 --> 00:58:05,723
This 50-g screening test is followed by a diagnostic 100-g, 3-hour oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) if screening results meet or exceed a predetermined plasma glucose concentration.

405
00:58:05,723 --> 00:58:08,223
Table 60-11.

406
00:58:08,223 --> 00:58:24,623
Threshold Values for Diagnosis of Gestational Diabetes with 75-g OGTT For the 50-g screening test, the plasma glucose level is measured 1 hour after a 50-g oral glucose load without regard to the time of day or time of last meal.

407
00:58:24,623 --> 00:58:39,523
In an earlier review, the pooled sensitivity for a threshold of 140 mg/dL ranged from 74 to 83 percent depending on 100-g thresholds used for diagnosis (van Leeuwen, 2012).

408
00:58:39,523 --> 00:58:58,711
Sensitivity estimates for a 50-g screen threshold of 135 mg/dL improved only slightly to 78 to 85 percent, but specificity dropped from a range of 72 to 85 percent for 140 mg/dL to 65 to 81 percent.

409
00:58:58,711 --> 00:59:07,036
Using a threshold of 130 mg/dL marginally improves sensitivity with a further decline in specificity .

410
00:59:07,036 --> 00:59:20,698
In the absence of clear evidence supporting one cutoff value over another, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) sanctions using any one of these three 50-g screen thresholds.

411
00:59:20,698 --> 00:59:29,686
At Parkland Hospital, we continue to use 140 mg/dL as the screening threshold to prompt the 100-g test.

412
00:59:29,686 --> 00:59:36,798
The threshold used at the University of Alabama at Birmingham is 135 mg/dL.

413
00:59:36,798 --> 00:59:45,536
Justification for screening and treatment of women with gestational diabetes was strengthened by a randomized treatment trial in 1000 women .

414
00:59:45,536 --> 00:59:59,611
Women diagnosed with gestational diabetes after a 75-g OGTT and assigned to receive dietary advice with blood glucose monitoring plus insulin therapy were compared with a cohort assigned to usual prenatal care.

415
00:59:59,611 --> 01:00:09,586
The former group had a significantly lower risk of a composite perinatal adverse outcome that included death, shoulder dystocia, bone fracture, and nerve palsy.

416
01:00:09,586 --> 01:00:21,561
Macrosomia defined by birthweight ≥4000 g complicated 10 percent of deliveries in the intervention group compared with 21 percent in the routine prenatal care group.

417
01:00:21,561 --> 01:00:26,386
Cesarean delivery rates were almost identical in the two study groups.

418
01:00:26,386 --> 01:00:39,873
Slightly different results were reported by the Maternal–Fetal Medicine Units Network randomized trial of 958 women identified with mild gestational diabetes using a two-step screening and diagnosis approach .

419
01:00:39,873 --> 01:00:51,348
They reported no differences in rates of composite morbidity that included stillbirth, birth trauma, and neonatal hypoglycemia, hyperinsulinemia, and hyperbilirubinemia.

420
01:00:51,348 --> 01:01:06,411
Secondary analyses did demonstrate a 50-percent reduction in macrosomia, fewer cesarean deliveries, and a significant decrease in shoulder dystocia rate—1.5 versus 4 percent—in treated versus routine care groups.

421
01:01:06,411 --> 01:01:18,698
Based largely on these two landmark studies, the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force (2021) recommends universal screening in low-risk women after 24 weeks’ gestation.

422
01:01:18,698 --> 01:01:27,798
However, the Task Force concluded that evidence is insufficient to assess the balance of benefits versus harms of screening before 24 weeks.

423
01:01:27,798 --> 01:01:32,323
Earlier screening in obese women did not result in better outcomes .

424
01:01:32,323 --> 01:01:37,948
The optimal OGTT to identify gestational diabetes also is debated.

425
01:01:37,948 --> 01:01:46,048
Proposed criteria for interpretation of the diagnostic 100-g OGTT are shown in Table 60-13.

426
01:01:46,048 --> 01:01:52,173
Criteria for the 75-g OGTT are shown in Table 60-11.

427
01:01:52,173 --> 01:02:05,761
A secondary analysis of the Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units Network treatment trial showed that women diagnosed with either the National Diabetes Data Group (NDDG) or the Carpenter-Coustan criteria benefited from treatment .

428
01:02:05,761 --> 01:02:12,623
However, the number needed to treat to prevent a shoulder dystocia was higher for the Carpenter-Coustan criteria.

429
01:02:12,623 --> 01:02:15,773
Others report similar observations .

430
01:02:15,773 --> 01:02:17,173
At

431
01:02:17,332 --> 01:02:27,919
Parkland Hospital, we use the NDDG criteria for diagnosis, whereas Carpenter-Coustan criteria are preferred at the University of Alabama at Birmingham.

432
01:02:27,869 --> 01:02:30,519
Table 60-13.

433
01:02:30,519 --> 01:02:58,094
Diagnosis of Gestational Diabetes Mellitus Using Threshold Glucose Values from 100-g Oral Glucose Tolerance Testa,b Controversies of Screening and Diagnosis The HAPO study was a 7-year international epidemiological study of 23,325 pregnant women at 15 centers in nine countries analyzing the association of various levels of glucose intolerance during the third trimester with adverse perinatal outcomes .

434
01:02:58,094 --> 01:03:02,507
Blood glucose levels were stratified into seven categories .

435
01:03:02,507 --> 01:03:15,782
These values were then correlated with rates for birthweight >90th percentile, primary cesarean delivery, neonatal hypoglycemia, and cord serum Cpeptide levels >90th percentile.

436
01:03:15,782 --> 01:03:23,632
Cord serum C-peptide is secreted in equimolar concentrations with insulin and reflects fetal β-islet cell function.

437
01:03:23,632 --> 01:03:31,619
Findings generally supported the supposition that increasing plasma glucose levels were associated with increasing adverse outcomes.

438
01:03:31,619 --> 01:03:34,007
Figure 60-4.

439
01:03:34,007 --> 01:03:38,844
Hyperglycemia and Adverse Pregnancy Outcome (HAPO) Study.

440
01:03:38,844 --> 01:03:52,169
The frequency of newborn birthweight ≥90th percentile for gestational age plotted against glucose levels fasting and at 1and 2-hr intervals following a 75-g oral glucose load.

441
01:03:52,169 --> 01:03:55,732
LGA = large for gestational age.

442
01:03:55,732 --> 01:04:13,257
Reproduced with permission from HAPO Study Cooperative Research Group, Metzger BE, Lowe LP, et al: Hyperglycemia and adverse pregnancy outcomes, N Engl J Med. 2008 May 8;358(19):1991–2002.

443
01:04:13,257 --> 01:04:24,182
The International Association of Diabetes and Pregnancy Study Group This workshop conference in 2008 concerned the diagnosis and classification of gestational diabetes.

444
01:04:24,182 --> 01:04:33,907
After reviewing the results of the HAPO study, a panel developed recommendations for the diagnosis and classification of hyperglycemia during pregnancy.

445
01:04:33,907 --> 01:04:40,957
This panel allowed for the diagnosis of overt diabetes during pregnancy as shown in Table 60-4.

446
01:04:40,957 --> 01:05:00,307
It also recommended a single-step approach to the diagnosis of gestational diabetes using the 75-g, 2-hour OGTT and thresholds derived using an arbitrary 1.75 odds ratio of outcomes such as LGA birthweight and cord serum C-peptide levels >90th percentile.

447
01:05:00,307 --> 01:05:09,369
Only one of these thresholds, shown in Table 60-11, would need to be met or exceeded to make the diagnosis of gestational diabetes.

448
01:05:09,369 --> 01:05:21,719
During their deliberations, the IADPSG estimated that implementation of these recommendations would raise the prevalence of gestational diabetes in the United States to 17.8 percent!

449
01:05:21,719 --> 01:05:31,432
Said another way, using this approach, the number of women with mild gestational diabetes would increase almost threefold with no evidence of treatment benefit .

450
01:05:31,432 --> 01:05:42,757
Feldman and coworkers (2016) evaluated the implementation of the IADPSG paradigm in a before-after analysis that included more than 6000 women.

451
01:05:42,757 --> 01:05:53,294
Compared with a two-step approach, the new strategy was associated with a significant rise in gestational diabetes diagnosis rates but not with reduced macrosomia rates.

452
01:05:53,294 --> 01:06:02,269
Remarkably, they identified a higher primary cesarean delivery rate associated with adoption of the IADPSG recommendations.

453
01:06:02,269 --> 01:06:16,957
The American Diabetes Association (2013, 2019) initially recommended adopting this new approach, based on benefits inferred from trials in women identified using a two-step approach described earlier.

454
01:06:16,957 --> 01:06:22,132
However, it now concludes that data also support a two-step strategy.

455
01:06:22,132 --> 01:06:35,494
NIH Consensus Development Conference Prompted by these disparate recommendations, the NIH Consensus Development Conference on Diagnosing Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (2013) was convened.

456
01:06:35,494 --> 01:06:45,907
The panel found insufficient evidence to adopt the one-step diagnostic process proposed by the IADPSG and recommended continuation of the two-step approach.

457
01:06:45,907 --> 01:06:56,569
The Consensus Conference panel did also suggest that further studies resolving the beneficial uncertainties associated with the one-step approach could warrant revision of their conclusions.

458
01:06:56,569 --> 01:07:05,194
A recent trial compared the IADPSG single-step approach to the more traditional two-step approach in almost 24,000 women .

459
01:07:05,194 --> 01:07:08,407
Consistent with the IADPSG

460
01:07:08,572 --> 01:07:20,459
prediction, the incidence of gestational diabetes was 16.5 percent in those who underwent single-step testing compared with 8.5 percent in women assigned to two-step testing.

461
01:07:20,409 --> 01:07:36,172
However, incidences of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, primary cesarean delivery, LGA newborns, and/or a perinatal composite outcome including measures of birth trauma and perinatal death were not materially different between testing groups.

462
01:07:36,172 --> 01:07:50,334
Thus, this large and pragmatic trial does not show maternal or perinatal benefit and thus does not justify the increased patient and healthcare costs of broadening the diagnosis of gestational diabetes using the proposed single-step approach .

463
01:07:50,334 --> 01:07:53,447
# Maternal and Fetal Effects.

464
01:07:53,447 --> 01:07:59,659
Adverse consequences of gestational diabetes differ from those of pregestational diabetes .

465
01:07:59,659 --> 01:08:10,422
Similar to women with overt diabetes, adverse maternal effects associated with gestational diabetes include a higher frequency of hypertension and cesarean delivery.

466
01:08:10,422 --> 01:08:27,997
Unlike women with overt diabetes, women with gestational diabetes do not appear to have fetuses with substantially higher rates of anomalies than the general obstetrical population (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Sheffield, 2002).

467
01:08:27,997 --> 01:08:43,422
In a study of more than 1 million women from the Swedish Medical Birth Registry, major malformation rates were marginally elevated in fetuses of gestational diabetics compared with those of nondiabetic controls—2.3 versus 1.8 percent .

468
01:08:43,422 --> 01:08:47,309
From this Registry, the stillbirth rate was not higher.

469
01:08:47,309 --> 01:08:58,722
Additionally, the stillbirth rate was not increased in an analysis of more than 800,000 pregnancies from 2005 through 2011 (Jovanovič, 2015).

470
01:08:58,722 --> 01:09:07,972
That said, women with elevated fasting glucose levels have elevated rates of unexplained stillbirths similar to those of women with overt diabetes.

471
01:09:07,972 --> 01:09:18,297
This increasing risk with progressive maternal hyperglycemia emphasizes the importance of identifying women with evidence of preexisting diabetes early in pregnancy .

472
01:09:18,297 --> 01:09:29,697
Fetal Macrosomia The primary effect attributed to gestational diabetes is excessive fetal size that is variably defined and discussed in Chapter 47 (p. 832).

473
01:09:29,697 --> 01:09:37,797
The perinatal goal is to avoid difficult delivery from macrosomia and concomitant birth trauma associated with shoulder dystocia.

474
01:09:37,797 --> 01:09:54,509
In one analysis of more than 80,000 vaginal births in Chinese women, the calculated risk for shoulder dystocia in newborns weighing ≥4200 g was 76-fold greater than the risk in those weighing <3500 g .

475
01:09:54,509 --> 01:10:00,159
However, the odds ratio for shoulder dystocia in women with diabetes was <2.

476
01:10:00,159 --> 01:10:10,084
Said another way, although gestational diabetes is certainly a risk factor, it accounts for only a small number of pregnancies complicated by shoulder dystocia.

477
01:10:10,084 --> 01:10:24,372
One study identified shoulder dystocia in approximately 4 percent of women with mild gestational diabetes compared with <1 percent of women with a 50-g glucose screen result <120 mg/dL .

478
01:10:24,372 --> 01:10:38,722
The excessive shoulder and trunk fat that commonly characterizes the macrosomic newborn of a diabetic mother predisposes such neonates to shoulder dystocia or cesarean delivery (Durnwald, 2004; McFarland, 2000).

479
01:10:38,722 --> 01:10:52,059
However, one prospective study of fetal adipose measurements demonstrated no differences between measurements in 630 offspring of women with gestational diabetes and 142 without diabetes .

480
01:10:52,059 --> 01:10:57,947
The authors attributed this negative finding to successful treatment of gestational diabetes.

481
01:10:57,947 --> 01:11:02,084
Insulin-like growth factors also influence fetal growth.

482
01:11:02,084 --> 01:11:11,022
These proinsulin-like polypeptides are produced by virtually all fetal organs and are potent stimulators of cell differentiation and division.

483
01:11:11,022 --> 01:11:19,484
Although not a current clinical tool, one study found that maternal insulin-like growth factor 1 levels strongly correlated with birthweight .

484
01:11:19,484 --> 01:11:33,222
The HAPO Study Cooperative Research Group (2008) also found dramatic elevations in cord serum C-peptide levels associated with rising maternal glucose levels following a 75-g OGTT.

485
01:11:33,222 --> 01:11:40,622
C-peptide levels >90th percentile were found in almost a third of newborns in the highest glucose categories.

486
01:11:40,622 --> 01:11:45,472
Other factors implicated in macrosomia include epidermal growth

487
01:11:45,628 --> 01:11:58,403
factor, fibroblast growth factor, platelet-derived growth factor, leptin, and adiponectin (Grissa, 2010; Loukovaara, 2004; Mazaki-Tovi, 2005).

488
01:11:58,353 --> 01:12:11,453
Maternal body mass index (BMI) is an independent and more substantial risk factor for fetal macrosomia than is glucose intolerance (Ehrenberg, 2004; Mission, 2013).

489
01:12:11,453 --> 01:12:25,203
In a secondary analysis of women with either untreated mild gestational diabetes or normal glucose tolerance testing results, higher BMI levels were associated with rising birthweight, regardless of glucose levels .

490
01:12:25,203 --> 01:12:36,715
Another analysis found that gestational diabetes, compared with obesity or gestational weight gain, contributed the least to the population-attributable fraction of LGA neonates .

491
01:12:36,715 --> 01:12:43,915
The highest fraction of LGA neonates was attributable to maternal obesity plus excessive gestational weight gain.

492
01:12:43,915 --> 01:12:55,978
Similarly, Egan and colleagues (2014) found that excessive gestational weight gain is common in women with gestational diabetes and confers an additive risk for fetal macrosomia.

493
01:12:55,978 --> 01:13:03,903
Weight distribution also seems to play a role because the risk of gestational diabetes is greater with maternal truncal obesity.

494
01:13:03,903 --> 01:13:16,203
One cohort study found that increased maternal abdominal subcutaneous fat thickness at 18 to 22 weeks’ gestation correlated with BMI and was a better predictor of gestational diabetes .

495
01:13:16,203 --> 01:13:27,715
Neonatal Hypoglycemia Hyperinsulinemia may provoke severe neonatal hypoglycemia within minutes of birth, but only three fourths of these episodes occur in the first 6 hours .

496
01:13:27,715 --> 01:13:37,503
The definition of neonatal hypoglycemia is debated, and recommended clinical thresholds range from 35 to 45 mg/dL.

497
01:13:37,503 --> 01:13:48,753
One NIH workshop conference on the topic supported a threshold of 35 mg/dL in term newborns but cautioned that this practice is not strictly evidence based .

498
01:13:48,753 --> 01:14:02,565
Treating neonates with glucose levels <47 mg/dL did not improve outcomes compared with treating those with glucose levels <36 mg/dL (van Kempen, 2020).

499
01:14:02,565 --> 01:14:16,828
Newborns described by the HAPO Study Cooperative Research Group (2008) had an incidence of clinical neonatal hypoglycemia that rose with increasing maternal OGTT result values defined in Figure 60-4.

500
01:14:16,828 --> 01:14:28,115
The frequency varied from 1 to 2 percent, but it was as high as 4.6 percent in women with fasting glucose levels ≥100 mg/dL.

501
01:14:28,115 --> 01:14:49,378
Similarly, an analysis of more than 3000 Korean women who underwent a 50-g OGTT found that neonates born to women with a screening result ≥200 mg/dL were 84 times more likely to have hypoglycemia than those born to women with a result <140 mg/dL .

502
01:14:49,378 --> 01:14:55,503
The risk of neonatal hypoglycemia correlates with umbilical cord C-peptide levels.

503
01:14:55,503 --> 01:15:01,590
The risk also rises with birthweight, independent of a maternal diabetes diagnosis .

504
01:15:01,590 --> 01:15:03,815
# Management.

505
01:15:03,815 --> 01:15:10,803
Women with gestational diabetes can be divided into two functional classes using fasting glucose levels.

506
01:15:10,803 --> 01:15:27,590
Pharmacological methods are usually recommended if diet modification does not consistently maintain the fasting plasma glucose levels <95 mg/dL or the 2-hour postprandial plasma glucose <120 mg/dL .

507
01:15:27,590 --> 01:15:34,703
Whether pharmacological treatment should be used in women with lesser degrees of fasting hyperglycemia is unclear.

508
01:15:34,703 --> 01:15:41,090
No controlled trials have been done to identify ideal glucose targets for fetal risk prevention.

509
01:15:41,090 --> 01:15:52,815
On the other hand, the HAPO Study Cooperative Research Group (2008) did demonstrate increased fetal risk at glucose levels below the threshold used for diagnosis of diabetes.

510
01:15:52,815 --> 01:16:02,603
The Fifth International Workshop Conference recommended that fasting capillary glucose levels be kept ≤95 mg/dL .

511
01:16:02,603 --> 01:16:16,778
In their systematic review, Hartling and associates (2013) concluded that treating gestational diabetes resulted in significantly lower incidences of preeclampsia, shoulder dystocia, and macrosomia.

512
01:16:16,778 --> 01:16:25,440
For example, the calculated risk ratio was 0.50 for delivering a newborn >4000 g after treatment.

513
01:16:25,440 --> 01:16:34,265
These investigators caution that the attributed risk for these outcomes is low, especially when glucose values are only moderately elevated.

514
01:16:34,265 --> 01:16:41,853
They were unable to demonstrate an effect on neonatal hypoglycemia or on future metabolic outcomes in the offspring.

515
01:16:41,853 --> 01:16:45,178
Diabetic Diet Nutritional instructions

516
01:16:45,340 --> 01:16:52,327
generally include a carbohydrate-controlled diet sufficient to maintain normoglycemia and avoid ketosis.

517
01:16:52,277 --> 01:16:59,202
On average, this includes a caloric intake of 30 to 35 kcal/kg/d.

518
01:16:59,202 --> 01:17:13,115
In one study, 152 women with gestational diabetes were randomly assigned to a 40- or a 55-percent daily carbohydrate diet, and no differences in insulin levels and pregnancy outcomes were found .

519
01:17:13,115 --> 01:17:24,777
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) suggests that carbohydrate intake be limited to 33 to 40 percent of the total daily calories.

520
01:17:24,777 --> 01:17:30,902
The remaining calories are apportioned to give 20 percent as protein and 40 percent as fat.

521
01:17:30,902 --> 01:17:37,277
The most appropriate dietary approach for women with gestational diabetes has not been established.

522
01:17:37,277 --> 01:17:51,377
One metaanalysis of trials of lowglycemic-index diets found that diets higher in complex carbohydrates and dietary fiber reduced the risk of macrosomia and likelihood of insulin use in women with gestational diabetes .

523
01:17:51,377 --> 01:17:57,652
However, there clearly are limits to what can be accomplished with various dietary approaches alone.

524
01:17:57,652 --> 01:18:08,127
Most and Langer (2012) found that insulin was effective in reducing the risk of excessive birthweight in offspring of obese women with gestational diabetes.

525
01:18:08,127 --> 01:18:20,840
Casey and colleagues (2015b) also found that dietary treatment alone for morbidly obese women with mild gestational diabetes did not reduce neonatal fat mass or LGA birthweights.

526
01:18:20,840 --> 01:18:27,552
Exercise Few trials have evaluated exercise specifically for women with gestational diabetes.

527
01:18:27,552 --> 01:18:42,565
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends regular physical activity that incorporates aerobic and strengthconditioning exercise during pregnancy and extends this to women with gestational diabetes.

528
01:18:42,565 --> 01:18:58,902
Two recent metaanalyses demonstrate that structured exercise programs during pregnancy diminish weight gain during pregnancy and even reduce the risk of developing gestational diabetes (Russo, 2015; Sanabria-Martinez, 2015).

529
01:18:58,902 --> 01:19:05,127
Exercise during pregnancy in women with gestational diabetes also lowers glucose levels .

530
01:19:05,127 --> 01:19:26,627
Glucose Monitoring Hawkins and associates (2008) compared outcomes in 315 women with diettreated gestational diabetes who used personal glucose monitors against those of 615 gestational diabetics who were also diet-treated but who underwent intermittent fasting glucose evaluation during weekly obstetrical visits.

531
01:19:26,627 --> 01:19:33,002
Women using daily blood-glucose self-monitoring had significantly fewer macrosomic newborns.

532
01:19:33,002 --> 01:19:38,990
They also gained less weight after diagnosis than women evaluated during clinic visits only.

533
01:19:38,990 --> 01:19:46,777
These findings support the common practice of blood-glucose self-monitoring for women with diettreated gestational diabetes.

534
01:19:46,777 --> 01:19:52,977
Postprandial surveillance for gestational diabetes is superior to preprandial surveillance .

535
01:19:52,977 --> 01:20:05,302
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) and the American Diabetes Association (2019) recommend glucose assessment four times daily.

536
01:20:05,302 --> 01:20:11,540
The first check is performed fasting, and the remainder are done 1 or 2 hours after each meal.

537
01:20:11,540 --> 01:20:27,715
At Parkland Hospital in women with diet-treated gestational diabetes, changing to postprandial monitoring significantly reduced weekly maternal weight gain (0.45 lb) compared with preprandial monitoring (0.63 lb).

538
01:20:27,715 --> 01:20:39,790
Insulin Treatment Historically, insulin has been considered standard therapy in women with gestational diabetes when target glucose levels cannot be consistently achieved through nutrition and exercise.

539
01:20:39,790 --> 01:20:45,915
Insulin does not cross the placenta, and tight glycemic control can usually be achieved.

540
01:20:45,915 --> 01:20:54,840
Insulin therapy is typically added if fasting levels persist above 95 mg/dL in women with gestational diabetes.

541
01:20:54,840 --> 01:21:13,377
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) also recommends that insulin be considered in women with 1-hour postprandial levels that persistently exceed 140 mg/dL or those with 2-hour levels >120 mg/dL.

542
01:21:13,377 --> 01:21:20,540
Importantly, all of these thresholds are extrapolated from recommendations for managing women with overt diabetes.

543
01:21:20,540 --> 01:21:29,315
The starting insulin dose is typically 0.7 to 1.0 U/kg/d and is given in divided doses .

544
01:21:29,315 --> 01:21:33,677
A combination of intermediate-acting and short-acting insulin

545
01:21:33,844 --> 01:21:40,044
may be used, and dose adjustments are based on glucose levels at particular times of the day.

546
01:21:39,994 --> 01:21:49,206
At Parkland Hospital, the starting daily dose is divided so that two thirds is given in the morning before breakfast and one third in the evening before dinner.

547
01:21:49,206 --> 01:21:57,119
In the morning dose, one third is regular insulin and two thirds are NPH (neutral protamine Hagedorn) insulin.

548
01:21:57,119 --> 01:22:02,794
For the evening dose, one half is regular insulin and the other half is NPH.

549
01:22:02,794 --> 01:22:10,169
Insulin instruction for these women is accomplished either in a specialized outpatient clinic or during a short hospital stay.

550
01:22:10,169 --> 01:22:19,106
At the University of Alabama at Birmingham, a basal-bolus approach using insulin glargine with rapid-acting insulin at each meal is preferred.

551
01:22:19,106 --> 01:22:32,631
After calculating an initial insulin dose, half is given as long-acting glargine at bedtime, and the other half is administered as rapid-acting insulin split into three doses given before breakfast, lunch, and dinner.

552
01:22:32,631 --> 01:22:45,069
As shown in Table 608, when using insulin analogues such as insulin aspart and insulin lispro, the more rapid onset of action must be considered during postprandial glucose management.

553
01:22:45,069 --> 01:23:02,406
Oral Hypoglycemic Agents Insulin is the preferred first-line agent for persistent hyperglycemia in women with gestational diabetes (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; American Diabetes Association, 2017b).

554
01:23:02,406 --> 01:23:18,456
Both organizations acknowledge that several studies support the safety and efficacy of either metformin (Glucophage) or glyburide (Micronase), which is also called glibenclamide (Feig, 2020; Langer, 2000; Nicholson, 2009).

555
01:23:18,456 --> 01:23:27,319
Balsells and coworkers (2015) completed a metaanalyses of trials that compared both agents with insulin or with each other.

556
01:23:27,319 --> 01:23:38,006
In the seven trials comparing glyburide with insulin, glyburide was associated with higher birthweight, more macrosomia, and more frequent neonatal hypoglycemia.

557
01:23:38,006 --> 01:23:49,094
In the six trials comparing metformin with insulin, metformin was associated with less maternal weight gain, more preterm birth, and less severe neonatal hypoglycemia.

558
01:23:49,094 --> 01:23:55,206
Women requiring insulin initiation or insulin addition were considered to have failed treatment.

559
01:23:55,206 --> 01:24:03,569
On average, such failures occurred in 6 percent of women treated with glyburide and 34 percent of those treated with metformin.

560
01:24:03,569 --> 01:24:12,044
In the two studies directly comparing oral hypoglycemic agents, however, treatment failure rates of both agents were equivalent.

561
01:24:12,044 --> 01:24:26,706
Moreover, reminiscent of findings from the trial of adjunctive metformin therapy in women with type 2 diabetes previously described, metformin treatment was associated with less maternal weight gain, lower birthweight, and less macrosomia.

562
01:24:26,706 --> 01:24:46,031
Conversely, in a randomized trial of glyburide treatment as an adjunct to diet therapy in 395 women with mild gestational diabetes, Casey and colleagues (2015a) did not identify any significant improvements in pregnancy outcomes in women treated with adjunctive glyburide.

563
01:24:46,031 --> 01:24:52,381
Concerns have also emerged regarding potential adverse outcomes among women treated with glyburide.

564
01:24:52,381 --> 01:25:00,381
First, glyburide crosses the placenta and reaches concentrations in the fetus that are more than two thirds of maternal levels .

565
01:25:00,381 --> 01:25:17,056
In a study of more than 9000 women with gestational diabetes treated with either insulin or glyburide, a significant rise in rates of neonatal intensive care unit admission, respiratory distress, and neonatal hypoglycemia was associated with glyburide use .

566
01:25:17,056 --> 01:25:23,369
Similarly, metformin reaches fetal serum concentrations nearly equal to maternal levels.

567
01:25:23,369 --> 01:25:45,194
However, in one study of 751 women with gestational diabetes who were randomly assigned to metformin or insulin treatment, short-term perinatal adverse events such as neonatal hypoglycemia, respiratory distress syndrome, phototherapy, or birth trauma did not differ between groups (Rowan, 2008; 2011).

568
01:25:45,194 --> 01:25:51,569
The fat distribution in children exposed to metformin showed a tendency toward a more favorable pattern.

569
01:25:51,569 --> 01:25:59,806
From a smaller randomized metformin trial, at 18 months, offspring exposed as fetuses to metformin were slightly heavier.

570
01:25:59,806 --> 01:26:05,881
However, markers of early motor or language development did not differ compared with those in

571
01:26:06,052 --> 01:26:11,289
offspring exposed as fetuses to insulin (Ijäs, 2015).

572
01:26:11,239 --> 01:26:18,777
The Food and Drug Administration has not approved glyburide or metformin use for treatment of gestational diabetes.

573
01:26:18,777 --> 01:26:30,864
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recognizes both as reasonable choices for second-line glycemic control in women with gestational diabetes.

574
01:26:30,864 --> 01:26:38,952
Because long-term outcomes have not been fully studied, the College recommends disclosure of the limitations in current safety data.

575
01:26:38,952 --> 01:26:41,752
# Obstetrical Management.

576
01:26:41,752 --> 01:26:50,927
In general, for women with gestational diabetes who do not require insulin, early delivery or other interventions are seldom required.

577
01:26:50,927 --> 01:26:56,452
There is no consensus regarding the value or timing of antepartum fetal testing.

578
01:26:56,452 --> 01:27:02,702
It is typically reserved for women with pregestational diabetes because of the greater stillbirth risk.

579
01:27:02,702 --> 01:27:15,077
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a, 2020c) endorses fetal surveillance in women with gestational diabetes and poor glycemic control.

580
01:27:15,077 --> 01:27:23,939
At Parkland Hospital, women with gestational diabetes are routinely instructed to perform daily fetal kick counts in the third trimester.

581
01:27:23,939 --> 01:27:31,352
As previously discussed, insulintreated women are offered inpatient admission after 34 weeks’ gestation.

582
01:27:31,352 --> 01:27:39,064
Approximately half of these women accept admission, and antepartum fetal monitoring is performed three times each week.

583
01:27:39,064 --> 01:27:45,114
Women with gestational diabetes and adequate glycemic control are managed expectantly.

584
01:27:45,114 --> 01:27:52,027
Elective labor induction to prevent shoulder dystocia compared with spontaneous labor remains controversial.

585
01:27:52,027 --> 01:28:06,477
In the truncated GINEXMAL randomized trial of 425 women with gestational diabetes, outcomes of labor induction between 38 and 39 weeks’ were compared with expectant management until 41 weeks’ gestation .

586
01:28:06,477 --> 01:28:19,264
Although underpowered, this trial demonstrated no clinically meaningful difference in the cesarean delivery rate between the induction and expectantly managed groups—12.6 versus 11.8 percent.

587
01:28:19,264 --> 01:28:29,789
However, with early labor induction, neonatal hyperbilirubinemia rates were significantly higher, and there was a nonsignificant threefold greater shoulder dystocia rate.

588
01:28:29,789 --> 01:28:46,052
In a cohort study of 8392 women with gestational diabetes, routine delivery at 38 or 39 weeks’ gestation was associated with a lower rate of cesarean delivery but with an elevated rate of neonatal intensive care unit admission .

589
01:28:46,052 --> 01:28:59,439
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recommends that routine labor induction in women with diet-treated gestational diabetes should not occur before 39 weeks’ gestation.

590
01:28:59,439 --> 01:29:06,739
As mentioned previously, at Parkland Hospital, those treated with insulin are delivered at 38 weeks’ gestation.

591
01:29:06,739 --> 01:29:13,089
At the University of Alabama at Birmingham, delivery is carried out after 39 weeks.

592
01:29:13,089 --> 01:29:19,852
Elective cesarean delivery to avoid brachial plexus injuries in overgrown fetuses is another issue.

593
01:29:19,852 --> 01:29:59,227
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) concludes that data are insufficient to determine whether women with gestational diabetes whose fetuses have a sonographically estimated weight ≥4500 g should undergo cesarean delivery to avoid risk of birth trauma. In one systematic review, Garabedian and associates (2010) estimated that as many as 588 cesarean deliveries in women with gestational diabetes and an estimated fetal weight of ≥4500 g would be necessary to avoid one case of permanent brachial plexus palsy.

594
01:29:59,227 --> 01:30:10,877
In one analysis of 903 women with gestational diabetes, sonographic estimates of fetal weight within 1 month of delivery typically overdiagnosed fetuses as being LGA.

595
01:30:10,877 --> 01:30:17,777
Only 22 percent of women estimated to have an LGA fetus actually delivered an overgrown newborn .

596
01:30:17,777 --> 01:30:35,639
Still, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) acknowledges that prophylactic cesarean delivery may be considered in diabetic women with an estimated fetal weight ≥4500 g. # Postpartum Evaluation.

597
01:30:35,639 --> 01:30:48,052
Recommendations for postpartum evaluation are based on the 50- to 75percent likelihood that women with gestational diabetes will develop overt diabetes within 15 to 25 years .

598
01:30:48,052 --> 01:30:51,452
The Fifth International Workshop Conference

599
01:30:51,604 --> 01:31:01,679
on Gestational Diabetes recommended that women diagnosed with gestational diabetes undergo postpartum evaluation with a 75-g OGTT .

600
01:31:01,629 --> 01:31:09,804
These recommendations and the classification scheme of the American Diabetes Association are shown in Table 60-14.

601
01:31:09,804 --> 01:31:23,004
However, one study of insurance claim data from 2000 to 2013 showed that only 24 percent of women with a pregnancy complicated by gestational diabetes underwent postpartum screening within a year.

602
01:31:23,004 --> 01:31:27,491
Less than half of those underwent a 75-g OGTT .

603
01:31:27,491 --> 01:31:43,141
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recommends either a fasting glucose assessment or a 75-g, 2-hour OGTT at 4 to 12 weeks postpartum for the diagnosis of overt diabetes.

604
01:31:43,141 --> 01:31:55,079
The American Diabetes Association (2019) recommends testing every 1 to 3 years in women with a history of gestational diabetes but normal postpartum glucose screening.

605
01:31:55,079 --> 01:31:57,629
Table 60-14.

606
01:31:57,629 --> 01:32:16,904
Fifth International Workshop-Conference: Metabolic Assessments Recommended After Pregnancy with Gestational Diabetes Women with a history of gestational diabetes are also at risk for cardiovascular complications associated with dyslipidemia, hypertension, and abdominal obesity—the metabolic syndrome .

607
01:32:16,904 --> 01:32:30,054
In a study of 47,909 parous women, the nearly 5000 women with prior gestational diabetes were 2.6 times more likely to be hospitalized for cardiovascular morbidity .

608
01:32:30,054 --> 01:32:38,729
Another study evaluated 483 women between 5 and 10 years after being diagnosed with mild gestational diabetes .

609
01:32:38,729 --> 01:32:45,704
Investigators found no increased risk for developing metabolic syndrome associated with additional pregnancies.

610
01:32:45,704 --> 01:32:54,679
However, risk for subsequent diabetes rose almost fourfold if gestational diabetes complicated at least one subsequent pregnancy.

611
01:32:54,679 --> 01:32:58,104
# Recurrent Gestational Diabetes.

612
01:32:58,104 --> 01:33:04,679
In one large metaanalysis, the pooled gestational diabetes recurrence rate was 48 percent.

613
01:33:04,679 --> 01:33:21,266
The same investigative group identified elevated maternal BMI, insulin use, fetal macrosomia, and weight gain between pregnancies as additional risk factors for gestational diabetes recurrence (Schwartz, 2015, 2016).

614
01:33:21,266 --> 01:33:30,304
Lifestyle behavioral changes that include weight control and exercise between pregnancies would seem likely to prevent gestational diabetes recurrence.

615
01:33:30,304 --> 01:33:40,054
However, women randomized to an exercise program that started before 14 weeks’ gestation in a subsequent pregnancy did not have a lower recurrence rate .

616
01:33:40,054 --> 01:33:54,666
Conversely, Ehrlich and coworkers (2011) found that prepregnancy loss of at least two BMI units was associated with a lower subsequent risk of gestational diabetes in women who were overweight or obese in the first pregnancy.

